{
  "id": "SPEC-002",
  "purpose": "Give callisto consumers a working, documented mechanism that places CI-built native binary artifacts (napi-rs .node files, maturin wheels) into their platform-stub package directories before `callisto publish` runs, closing the gap between `callisto matrix`'s output and a working end-to-end release.",
  "scope": "Extends `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s existing `callisto-orchestrate` composite-action step: within the same shell step that computes `NATIVE_MATRIX` (action.yml:164) and before it invokes `callisto plan-publish`/publish (in the branch already guarded by `INPUT_PUBLISH`), adds a placement loop that downloads each `nativeMatrix` entry's artifact by `artifactName`, pinned to the current workflow run via `$GITHUB_RUN_ID`, and places its contents into `packageDir`. Also updates `.claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md` §6.2's reference snippet to match this mechanism exactly, and corrects `ARCHITECTURE.md` §12's broken example workflow to remove the nonexistent `callisto publish-target` reference from the example code block and extend it through placement and publish.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Extending PlatformTargetKind to Java/JNI or .NET native-AOT targets -- a separate, later concern; this spec only routes the two kinds PlatformTargetKind already supports (Napi, Maturin).",
    "Prebuilt Rust CLI binaries distributed as GitHub Release assets (\"Tier 2\") -- a separate, related, deferred future spec.",
    "Homebrew tap formula generation/distribution -- deferred by the maintainer as a possible separate future tool.",
    "Go binary distribution -- already an existing non-goal per .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §8, not being revisited here.",
    "A generic callisto-build-action that dispatches the actual per-platform build (napi build --platform --release, etc.) -- callisto-matrix.md §7's secondary/optional proposal. This spec covers only placement of an already-built, correctly-named artifact; the build step itself is the consumer's own responsibility.",
    "Validating the content of a downloaded artifact (e.g. confirming a .node file is well-formed, or that it is actually a valid binary for its target triple). The placement step moves whatever files gh run download retrieves into packageDir; content correctness is the build job's responsibility and surfaces later, at publish or install time, not at placement.",
    "Adding a new required input to callisto-action to opt into placement. Placement is driven entirely by whether nativeMatrix is a non-empty array -- consumers with no native platform targets see zero behavior change.",
    "Implementing the nonexistent callisto publish-target Rust CLI subcommand referenced by ARCHITECTURE.md §12's status note (line 447). That subcommand is not needed for this requirement and is not built by this spec; the status note's reference to it (alongside Java/.NET native-AOT discovery) remains accurate and is deliberately left in place -- only the example workflow's own code block, which currently invokes the nonexistent subcommand directly, is corrected.",
    "Guaranteeing that a binary placed at packageDir by this step is picked up and included in callisto publish's registry upload output (npm tarball, PyPI wheel, etc.). That round-trip guarantee is owned by whatever spec governs callisto publish's own native-binary packaging logic (crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs) -- this spec's guarantee ends at the file being present on disk at packageDir before publish begins. If no spec currently covers that round-trip, closing it is a follow-up dependency of this spec, not an implicit assumption made here.",
    "Handling malformed or invalid-JSON nativeMatrix values. NATIVE_MATRIX (action.yml:164) is produced and consumed entirely within one shell step under `set -o pipefail`, with `jq` already validating JSON syntax (and failing the step via `|| { echo \"::error::callisto matrix failed\"; exit 1; }`) before the placement loop added by this spec ever reads the value. There is no cross-job boundary, no external caller, and no hand-edit surface between production and consumption of NATIVE_MATRIX -- malformed JSON cannot reach the placement loop by construction.",
    "Handling a missing (absent-key) or null artifactName/packageDir field on an otherwise-valid nativeMatrix entry. crates/callisto-model/src/matrix.rs's PlatformTarget declares both fields as plain `String` (not `Option<String>`), so serde can never serialize a missing or null value for either field into the JSON callisto matrix emits -- these states are unreachable by the type system, not merely unlikely.",
    "Re-verifying artifactName uniqueness within one invocation of the placement loop. The existing NATIVE_MATRIX computation already deduplicates via `unique_by(.artifactName)` (action.yml:164) before the placement loop added by this spec ever sees the array; two entries with the same artifactName cannot reach the loop."
  ],
  "api_surface": [
    {
      "name": "callisto-action native artifact placement loop",
      "signature": "New shell logic inside `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s existing `callisto-orchestrate` composite step, added immediately after `NATIVE_MATRIX` is computed (action.yml:164-165) and before `callisto plan-publish`/publish executes, inside the branch guarded by `else` (no pending changesets) and further guarded by `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` (matching action.yml:167's existing guard verbatim).",
      "description": "Feeds `NATIVE_MATRIX` through process substitution, not a pipe -- `while IFS= read -r ENTRY; do ... done < <(printf '%s' \"$NATIVE_MATRIX\" | jq -c '.[]')` -- matching the existing pattern already used for the releases loop at action.yml:219, so that an `exit 1` inside the loop body terminates the step itself rather than only a pipeline subshell. For each ENTRY: extracts `ARTIFACT=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.artifactName')` and `DIR_RAW=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.packageDir')`; normalizes `DIR_RAW` to `.` when it is the empty string (the real, reachable value `packageDir` takes for a workspace-root package, per crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs:34-39's `.parent()` on a root-level manifest path), otherwise uses `DIR_RAW` as-is, yielding `DIR`; runs `mkdir -p \"$DIR\"` explicitly (not relying on `gh run download`'s own directory-creation behavior, which is unversioned/unspecified upstream behavior this spec does not depend on); then runs `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\" || { echo \"::error::failed to download artifact '$ARTIFACT' for placement into '$DIR' within run $GITHUB_RUN_ID\"; exit 1; }`, where `$GITHUB_RUN_ID` (set automatically by GitHub Actions) pins the artifact lookup to the current workflow run so a same-named artifact left over from an older or unrelated run cannot be downloaded in its place. Runs once per array element, in array order. Skipped entirely (loop body never executes, zero `gh run download` invocations) when `NATIVE_MATRIX` is `[]`."
    }
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Given a workflow run where N per-platform build jobs each upload an artifact via actions/upload-artifact using the exact artifactName string from that platform's callisto matrix --format json entry, and the callisto-orchestrate step subsequently runs with INPUT_PUBLISH non-empty and not the literal string \"false\": after the step completes, each of the N artifacts' downloaded file contents are present under the corresponding packageDir path relative to the working directory (with the directory having been created via the loop's own mkdir -p if it did not already exist), and this placement completes before the step's callisto plan-publish/publish invocation begins.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Given nativeMatrix is [] (no native platform-target packages in the workspace) and INPUT_PUBLISH is non-empty and not the literal string \"false\", the callisto-orchestrate step's placement loop body never executes (zero gh run download invocations) and the step proceeds directly to callisto plan-publish/publish exactly as it did before this change.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose packageDir field is the empty string (the value produced for a workspace-root package whose manifest has no parent directory component), the placement loop normalizes it to \".\" before calling mkdir -p and gh run download --dir, and the corresponding artifact's contents are placed directly under the working directory rather than the step failing or attempting mkdir -p with an empty path.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose artifactName was never uploaded within the current workflow run ($GITHUB_RUN_ID) -- including the case where an artifact with that exact name exists in a different, older, or unrelated workflow run in the same repository -- the loop's `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\"` invocation exits non-zero because the run-id pins the lookup to the current run only, the `|| { ...; exit 1; }` clause terminates the step (not merely a pipeline subshell, since the loop is fed via process substitution) before reaching callisto plan-publish/publish, and the step's log output names the specific missing artifactName and its target packageDir.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example workflow code block (the fenced code block following the \"Zero-Config GitHub Actions Workflow Pattern\" heading) contains no occurrence of the string \"callisto publish-target\" anywhere within it; the status note above the heading (containing the sentence naming Java/JNI, .NET native-AOT, and the callisto publish-target subcommand as unimplemented) is unchanged and still present.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example workflow, read end to end, shows all of: a build job producing and uploading a per-platform artifact, a placement step downloading that artifact into its package directory, and a callisto publish (or callisto-action with publish enabled) step running afterward against the populated package directories -- not just the build step.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "A consumer workflow that already uses callisto-action for publishing and adds only a matrix-driven build job (per .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §6.1's nativeMatrix consumption pattern) uploading artifacts named per artifactName, with no additional bash, composite action, or script authored by the consumer to perform placement, ends with the callisto-orchestrate step having invoked gh run download once per nativeMatrix entry and each entry's artifact contents present at that entry's normalized packageDir path -- with no requirement on this spec's part that callisto publish itself subsequently succeeds, per this spec's non-goal on the publish-side packaging round-trip.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": ".claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §6.2's reference code snippet, read as currently written, matches this spec's actual mechanism: it pins the download to $GITHUB_RUN_ID, normalizes an empty packageDir to \".\", calls mkdir -p on the target directory explicitly, and is fed via process substitution rather than a pipe -- not the original un-pinned, no-mkdir, piped form the design doc previously specified.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-001",
  "reasoning": "Second revision, responding to canon:exit-gate's fail verdict (retry 1, 9 blockers, high confidence). Root cause the gate identified: several original criteria (invalid-JSON nativeMatrix, missing/null artifactName or packageDir) described states that are structurally unreachable given (a) the placement loop living inside the same atomic shell step that produces NATIVE_MATRIX (no cross-job boundary for tampering) and (b) PlatformTarget's non-Option String fields (serde cannot emit missing/null). Rather than relocate the loop into a separate consuming job (which would reintroduce real malformed-input surface but at the cost of restructuring the entire mechanism the drafter had already reasoned through and chosen deliberately), this revision keeps the in-step design and instead deletes the criteria that were never reachable in that design, converting them into explicit non_goals stating exactly why they're unreachable (traced to specific line numbers: action.yml:164's pipefail+jq guard, matrix.rs's String field types). One case the gate flagged as part of the same cluster was NOT unreachable and needed a real fix, not deletion: packageDir CAN be the empty string for a workspace-root package -- traced firsthand through crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs:34-39 (`.parent()` on a root-relative manifest path yields an empty PathBuf, stringified to \"\") -- and `mkdir -p \"\"` was empirically confirmed (via a local shell test) to fail with exit 1. This is now AC-003 (non-error, since it's the normal/expected root-package case, not a failure), with explicit `.`-normalization added to api_surface. AC-006 (old, artifactName-collision) was deleted rather than rewritten per the gate's own note that the suggested replacement wording had the identical reachability defect (the array reaching the loop is already unique_by-deduplicated upstream) -- explained in a new non_goal instead. AC-009 (old) was rewritten as AC-007, removing \"and callisto publish succeeding\" so it no longer contradicts the publish-round-trip non_goal. AC-001/002's \"truthy\" language was replaced with the exact guard predicate from action.yml:167. api_surface now specifies process substitution (not a pipe) and an explicit mkdir -p plus exit-terminating error handling per download, addressing the AC-003/AC-005(old) subshell-exit-swallowing finding directly in the mechanism description rather than leaving it to an implementer's guess. AC-007(old)/AC-005(new) narrows the callisto-publish-target ban to the example code block specifically, per the gate's first suggested option, since the status note's other claims (Java/.NET/the subcommand itself all still unimplemented) remain true and non-contradictory. New AC-008 closes the gate's final blocker: callisto-matrix.md §6.2's own reference snippet must be updated to match this spec's real mechanism, so the internal design doc and the shipped implementation don't silently diverge."
}

